WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.670
Telemetry is the operational evidence that shows what a system is doing, how well it is performing, and where it may be failing.

2
00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:16.470
In ordinary services, telemetry often means metrics, logs, traces, events, audit records, and health checks.

3
00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:22.880
Those signals help teams understand latency, errors, throughput, resource use, and important state changes.

4
00:00:23.130 --> 00:00:28.900
AI systems need those same signals, but they also need AI-specific context.

5
00:00:29.150 --> 00:00:39.210
A production inference path may depend on a model version, prompt template, retrieval index, data pipeline, accelerator, queue, cache, model gateway, and policy decision.

6
00:00:39.460 --> 00:00:51.470
If telemetry only says that an API was slow, the team still needs to know whether the delay came from model execution, retrieval, scheduling, storage, network, or another dependency.

7
00:00:51.720 --> 00:00:55.540
Hybrid and clustered environments make this more important.

8
00:00:55.790 --> 00:01:03.510
A single request can cross application code, orchestration layers, GPU nodes, managed services, private networks, and external APIs.

9
00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:10.310
Each layer can be healthy by itself while the combined workflow still fails for users.

10
00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:19.060
Useful telemetry connects application behavior to infrastructure behavior so engineering, operations, security, and governance teams can reason from shared evidence.

11
00:01:19.310 --> 00:01:22.740
Good telemetry is designed before incidents happen.

12
00:01:22.990 --> 00:01:27.980
It is not a pile of everything a platform could emit.

13
00:01:28.230 --> 00:01:42.190
It is a set of signals chosen because they answer operational questions: what happened, where did it happen, who or what was involved, how severe was it, and what should the team do next?

